  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe extended forecast period looks on track, with drier\nconditions over the weekend into Monday, with very hot\ntemperatures. An upper trough passing to the north will bring\nmore thunderstorm chances to southern Colorado for mid week.\n\nSaturday into Monday...the upper level high will retrograde\nsouthwest into Arizona on Saturday, before an upper trough in\nthe Pacific Northwest stretches a ridge from Arizona, northeast\ninto the Northern Plains for Sunday into Monday. \n\nOne last embedded upper wave in northerly flow aloft will\ntransit across Colorado on Saturday, while drier air moves in\nfrom the west. Shower and thunderstorm coverage looks minimal on\nSaturday, mainly confined to the San Juan and southern Sangre \nde Cristo Ranges during the afternoon and evening hours. Current\nCAM guidance keeps a low end risk of thunderstorms across the \nAspen Acres burn scar Saturday afternoon, but the bulk of the \nactivity looks to remain south. Any cells that do develop on or \nnear the burn scar could produce flash flooding, but confidence \nis low in this occuring at this time. Showers and thunderstorms \nwill quickly drop south into New Mexico by the evening, with dry\nconditions overnight. Overall, temperatures on Saturday look \nwarm, with 80s and lower 90s across the lower elevations.\n\nBy Sunday and Monday, the upper ridge will stretch across\nColorado ahead of an upper trough across the Pacific Northwest.\nDrier air spreads across Colorado, and temperatures will warm\nacross the region. Kept the forecast mainly dry Sunday into\nMonday, with just isolated activity over the higher terrain on\nMonday afternoon evening. Temperatures will be the biggest\nissue, with afternoon high temperatures in the upper 90s to\nlower 100s Sunday, and the warmest day forecast on Monday. At\nthis time, heat highlights may be needed for parts of the area.\nOvernight lows will also remain mild with 60s and 70s.\n\nTuesday through Friday...the Pacific Northwest upper trough will\nslowly move into the Northern Plains, flattening the upper ridge\nacross the Desert Southwest, and producing persistent westerly\nflow aloft across Colorado. A strong cold front arrives Tuesday\nmorning, with increasing moisture and easterly upslope flow.\nPWAT values increase significantly to in excess of 1 inch on\nTuesday, and easterly upslope flow will lead to thunderstorm\ndevelopment along the Eastern Mountains Tuesday afternoon and\nevening. Flash flood potential for area burn scars looks to\nincrease significantly on Tuesday. Very similar conditions\npersist on Wednesday, with another round of thunderstorms\ncapable of producing heavy rainfall, especially across the\nEastern Mountains.  \n\nBy Thursday and Friday, the upper trough tracks into the Great\nLakes, and high pressure begins to rebuild across the western\nstates. Multiple waves under the ridge will keep thunderstorm\nchances going through the end of the week for much of southern\nColorado.",
